\begin{abstract}
This paper substantiates Two-View Spatial Triangulation through software verification and physical validation. This work reconstructs three-dimensional (3D) points and trajectories using Direct Linear Transformation (DLT) formulation. The algorithmic pipeline combines isotropic mean-distance normalization, DLT camera calibration, user-activated Brown–Conrady
lens-distortion correction using user-provided coefficients, and inhomogeneous dual-view least-squares triangulation (solved by Householder QR). The software pipeline implements these numerical operations into an offline system for high frame-rate video analysis, extending 3D-modeling. 

Two-view reconstruction from optical video is sensitive to calibration geometry, lens distortion, image-point error, and the intersection angle of the viewing rays. This paper presents a computational pipeline that combines isotropic mean-distance normalization, Direct Linear Transformation (DLT) camera calibration, compact Householder QR reduction followed by Jacobi singular value decomposition (SVD), Brown--Conrady distortion inversion, and inhomogeneous least-squares triangulation solved with Householder QR. Unlike a normal-equation solve, the reconstruction stage does not explicitly form $M^{T}M$. Calibration diagnostics distinguish the non-null subspace conditioning from the singular-value gap associated with the estimated null vector. Point-level checks use a spectral condition estimate, a ray-intersection angle, and reprojection error; thresholds are treated as application-dependent acceptance policies rather than universal guarantees. Reported software comparison tests show close numerical agreement between Java and TypeScript implementations, while a preliminary synthetic benchmark illustrates the growth of depth error as the ray angle narrows. These results establish numerical consistency under the tested conditions, not physical accuracy. A reproducible test-range validation protocol using surveyed control points, synchronized exposures, and an independent dynamic reference is specified for future measurement of metric accuracy and trajectory uncertainty.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Two-view reconstruction, stereo triangulation, Direct Linear Transformation, Householder QR, camera calibration, lens distortion, numerical conditioning, optical range tracking.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
Recovering metric three-dimensional (3-D) trajectories from two video views is relevant to optical range instrumentation, motion measurement, and dynamic test analysis. The reconstruction is determined not only by the triangulation equations but also by camera calibration, lens-distortion correction, correspondence error, temporal association, and viewing geometry \cite{hz,abdkarara}. When the camera rays intersect at a small angle, modest image-plane errors can produce large uncertainty along depth. A numerically stable linear solve cannot remove this geometric ambiguity, so numerical diagnostics and geometric acceptance tests are both required.

A common least-squares implementation forms the normal equations $M^TM\X=M^T\mathbf{b}$. Although convenient, this transforms the 2-norm condition number according to $\kappa_2(M^TM)=\kappa_2(M)^2$ when $M$ has full column rank, potentially worsening finite-precision effects \cite{golub}. Calibration from unnormalized image and world coordinates can also be poorly scaled because pixel coordinates and metric coordinates occupy different numerical ranges \cite{hz}. These issues motivate normalization and orthogonal factorization at both the calibration and reconstruction stages.

This paper develops and evaluates a two-view pipeline with four principal contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item an isotropically normalized DLT calibration procedure using a compact Householder QR factorization and Jacobi SVD to estimate the camera null vector;
  \item distortion-aware observation preprocessing and a direct $4\times3$ inhomogeneous triangulation solve using Householder QR, without explicitly constructing normal equations;
  \item calibration- and point-level quality diagnostics that separate non-null calibration conditioning, null-space separation, reconstruction conditioning, ray angle, and reprojection residual;
  \item an initial numerical comparison and a test-range validation protocol that explicitly separates software agreement and synthetic sensitivity from physical measurement accuracy.
\end{enumerate}

The method is intended for the computer-vision and image/video-analysis side of optical range tracking. It does not claim that numerical stability alone guarantees field accuracy; that claim requires independent physical validation.

\section{Method}

\subsection{Camera Model and Coordinate Normalization}
For a world point $\X_i=[X_i,Y_i,Z_i]^T$, define its homogeneous form $\X_{i,h}=[X_i,Y_i,Z_i,1]^T$. A pinhole camera is represented by $P\in\R^{3\times4}$ and satisfies
\begin{equation}
  \lambda_i\x_i=P\X_{i,h},\qquad \x_i=[u_i,v_i,1]^T,
  \label{eq:camera}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_i\ne0$. A projective camera has 11 independent degrees of freedom. For a stable DLT estimate, image and 3-D control coordinates are transformed to have zero centroid and a prescribed mean distance from the origin \cite{hz}.

Let $\boldsymbol{\mu}_2$ be the centroid of the image control points and $d_2$ their mean Euclidean distance from it. Set $s_2=\sqrt{2}/d_2$ and
\begin{equation}
T_2=\begin{bmatrix}s_2&0&-s_2\mu_u\\0&s_2&-s_2\mu_v\\0&0&1\end{bmatrix}.
\label{eq:t2}
\end{equation}
For the 3-D control points, let the centroid be $\boldsymbol{\mu}_3$ and the mean distance be $d_3$. With $s_3=\sqrt{3}/d_3$, use
\begin{equation}
T_3=\begin{bmatrix}
s_3&0&0&-s_3\mu_X\\
0&s_3&0&-s_3\mu_Y\\
0&0&s_3&-s_3\mu_Z\\
0&0&0&1
\end{bmatrix}.
\label{eq:t3}
\end{equation}
The normalized coordinates are $\tilde{\x}_i=T_2\x_i$ and $\tilde{\X}_{i,h}=T_3\X_{i,h}$. The control configuration must span 3-D adequately; six points is the algebraic minimum, not a recommendation for a robust noisy calibration. Calibration quality should be assessed from matrix diagnostics and held-out reprojection error rather than from point count alone.

\subsection{Normalized DLT Calibration}
Each normalized correspondence contributes two rows to the homogeneous DLT system:
\begin{align}
A_i^{(u)}&=[\tilde{\X}_{i,h}^{T}\quad\mathbf{0}_{1\times4}\quad-\tilde{u}_i\tilde{\X}_{i,h}^{T}],\\
A_i^{(v)}&=[\mathbf{0}_{1\times4}\quad\tilde{\X}_{i,h}^{T}\quad-\tilde{v}_i\tilde{\X}_{i,h}^{T}].
\label{eq:dltrows}
\end{align}
Stacking $n$ correspondences gives $A\mathbf{p}=\mathbf{0}$, $A\in\R^{2n\times12}$. The normalized projection matrix is obtained from the right singular vector associated with the smallest singular value. To reduce the size of the SVD, compute a thin Householder QR factorization
\begin{equation}
A=QR_A,\quad Q\in\R^{2n\times12},\quad R_A\in\R^{12\times12},
\label{eq:qrA}
\end{equation}
then apply a cyclic one-sided Jacobi SVD to $R_A$. Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, this reduction preserves the singular values and right singular vectors in exact arithmetic. The right singular vector $\mathbf{v}_{12}$ associated with the smallest singular value is reshaped into $\tilde{P}$, and the denormalized camera matrix is
\begin{equation}
P=T_2^{-1}\tilde{P}T_3.
\label{eq:denorm}
\end{equation}

Let $\sigma_1\ge\sigma_2\ge\cdots\ge\sigma_{12}$ denote the singular values of the calibration design matrix (equivalently, of $R_A$ in exact arithmetic). Because DLT estimates a null direction, the full ratio $\sigma_1/\sigma_{12}$ is not an appropriate stand-alone condition measure. We instead report the non-null-subspace ratio
\begin{equation}
\kappa_{\mathrm{nonnull}}=\frac{\sigma_1}{\sigma_{11}},\qquad
\rho_{\mathrm{null}}=\frac{\sigma_{11}}{\max(\sigma_{12},\epsilon)},
\label{eq:caldiag}
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon$ prevents division by zero in finite-precision reporting. The first quantity describes conditioning away from the estimated null direction; the second measures separation of the smallest singular value from the rest. Neither replaces reprojection validation. Calibration controls, held-out points, and image observations must be expressed in the same coordinate domain.

The inhomogeneous triangulation equations below use a camera gauge with $P_{34}=1$. Thus, after denormalization, each camera matrix is scaled by $P_{34}$ only when this coefficient is safely nonzero relative to $\|P\|_F$. If that gauge is numerically inadmissible, the calibration is flagged and the implementation must change gauge or use a homogeneous triangulation method; it must not divide by a near-zero coefficient silently.

\subsection{Lens-Distortion Compensation}
When lens distortion is enabled, observed pixels are first mapped through the camera intrinsic matrix $K$ to dimensionless image coordinates. Brown--Conrady radial and tangential distortion is modeled as \cite{brown}
\begin{align}
r^2&=x^2+y^2,\quad L_r=1+k_1r^2+k_2r^4+k_3r^6,\\
x_d&=xL_r+2p_1xy+p_2(r^2+2x^2),\\
y_d&=yL_r+p_1(r^2+2y^2)+2p_2xy.
\label{eq:brown}
\end{align}
Here $(x,y)$ are undistorted normalized coordinates and $(x_d,y_d)$ are distorted normalized coordinates. The intrinsic matrix and distortion coefficients must be known or estimated independently; an 11-degree-of-freedom projective DLT matrix alone does not identify physical lens-distortion coefficients. Define the tangential terms as $	au_x=2p_1xy+p_2(r^2+2x^2)$ and $	au_y=p_1(r^2+2y^2)+2p_2xy$. Starting from $(x^{(0)},y^{(0)})=(x_d,y_d)$, fixed-point inversion is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
x^{(m+1)}&=\frac{x_d-\tau_x(x^{(m)},y^{(m)})}{L_r(x^{(m)},y^{(m)})},\\
y^{(m+1)}&=\frac{y_d-\tau_y(x^{(m)},y^{(m)})}{L_r(x^{(m)},y^{(m)})}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:undistort}
\end{equation}
Iteration stops when the update and forward-model residual meet specified tolerances. Non-finite iterates, a radial factor close to zero, or failure to converge within the iteration limit cause the observation to be rejected. Coordinates are not silently clamped to an arbitrary radius because clipping can introduce systematic image-point bias. The recovered normalized point is mapped back to an undistorted pixel coordinate, which is used consistently for DLT fitting, triangulation, and reprojection checks. If raw distorted pixels are used for residual reporting instead, the forward distortion model must be applied to the reprojected point before calculating the residual.

\subsection{Inhomogeneous Householder QR Triangulation}
Let the two calibrated camera matrices be scaled to the $P_{34}=1$ gauge and expressed by the 11 parameters
\begin{equation}
P_j=\begin{bmatrix}
L_1^{(j)}&L_2^{(j)}&L_3^{(j)}&L_4^{(j)}\\
L_5^{(j)}&L_6^{(j)}&L_7^{(j)}&L_8^{(j)}\\
L_9^{(j)}&L_{10}^{(j)}&L_{11}^{(j)}&1
\end{bmatrix},\quad j\in\{1,2\}.
\end{equation}
For undistorted image observations $(u_j,v_j)$, the projective equations can be rearranged into $M\X=\mathbf{b}$, where $M\in\R^{4\times3}$ and
\begin{equation}
M=\begin{bmatrix}
u_1L_9^{(1)}-L_1^{(1)}&u_1L_{10}^{(1)}-L_2^{(1)}&u_1L_{11}^{(1)}-L_3^{(1)}\\
v_1L_9^{(1)}-L_5^{(1)}&v_1L_{10}^{(1)}-L_6^{(1)}&v_1L_{11}^{(1)}-L_7^{(1)}\\
u_2L_9^{(2)}-L_1^{(2)}&u_2L_{10}^{(2)}-L_2^{(2)}&u_2L_{11}^{(2)}-L_3^{(2)}\\
v_2L_9^{(2)}-L_5^{(2)}&v_2L_{10}^{(2)}-L_6^{(2)}&v_2L_{11}^{(2)}-L_7^{(2)}
\end{bmatrix},
\label{eq:M}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{b}=\begin{bmatrix}L_4^{(1)}-u_1\\L_8^{(1)}-v_1\\L_4^{(2)}-u_2\\L_8^{(2)}-v_2\end{bmatrix}.
\label{eq:b}
\end{equation}
Compute a thin Householder QR factorization $M=Q_MR_M$, with $Q_M\in\R^{4\times3}$ and $R_M\in\R^{3\times3}$. Then solve
\begin{equation}
R_M\hat{\X}=Q_M^T\mathbf{b}
\label{eq:qrx}
\end{equation}
by triangular back-substitution. The implementation never explicitly forms $M^TM$. A spectral 2-norm condition estimate is obtained from the singular values of the small triangular factor:
\begin{equation}
\kappaTwo=\frac{\sigma_{\max}(R_M)}{\sigma_{\min}(R_M)}.
\label{eq:kappam}
\end{equation}
A diagonal ratio $\diagratio=\max_i|(R_M)_{ii}|/\min_i|(R_M)_{ii}|$ may be retained as a cheap warning indicator, but it is not a general condition-number estimate and must not be used as the sole numerical test.

\subsection{Geometric and Reprojection Quality Gates}
The optical centre of camera $j$ is obtained by solving $A_j\mathbf{C}_j=-\mathbf{a}_{4,j}$ for $P_j=[A_j\mid\mathbf{a}_{4,j}]$; QR or SVD is preferred over Cramer's rule. For a reconstructed point, define $\mathbf{r}_j=\hat{\X}-\mathbf{C}_j$ and
\begin{equation}
\theta_{\mathrm{ray}}=\cos^{-1}\!\left(\operatorname{clip}\left(\frac{\mathbf{r}_1^T\mathbf{r}_2}{\|\mathbf{r}_1\|_2\|\mathbf{r}_2\|_2},-1,1\right)\right).
\label{eq:ray}
\end{equation}
Three checks are applied to each candidate point:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textit{Numerical conditioning:} reject when $R_M$ is rank deficient or $\kappaTwo$ exceeds a configured limit. A limit of $10^4$ is used as a provisional engineering policy in the implementation, not as a universal boundary between valid and invalid geometry.
  \item \textit{Ray angle:} reject when $\theta_{\mathrm{ray}}<15^\circ$ under the current range-tracking policy. The threshold must be tuned to the baseline, working volume, noise level, and required uncertainty.
  \item \textit{Reprojection:} project $\hat{\X}$ into both views and calculate $\delta_j=\|[u_j,v_j]^T-\pi(P_j\hat{\X}_h)\|_2$ in the undistorted pixel domain. Reject when either residual exceeds a chosen threshold (nominally $1.5$ pixels).
\end{enumerate}
Passing these checks indicates that a point is acceptable under the selected policy; it is not a statistical confidence interval or proof of a specified metric accuracy. For metrology, uncertainty should additionally be estimated from image-point covariance, calibration uncertainty, and the local triangulation Jacobian.

