\subsection{Trajectory Kinematics and Timing}
For accepted reconstructed points $\hat{\X}(t_k)$, velocity and acceleration may be estimated by central differences when samples are uniformly spaced:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{V}(t_k)&=\frac{\hat{\X}(t_{k+1})-\hat{\X}(t_{k-1})}{2\Delta t},\\
\mathbf{a}(t_k)&=\frac{\hat{\X}(t_{k+1})-2\hat{\X}(t_k)+\hat{\X}(t_{k-1})}{\Delta t^2}.
\label{eq:kin}
\end{align}
Both approximations have second-order truncation error for a sufficiently smooth trajectory and uniform sampling. For irregular timestamps, the uniform-step formulas must be replaced by nonuniform finite differences or a local polynomial fit. Acceleration estimates amplify pixel and reconstruction noise; smoothing, uncertainty propagation, and the selected timebase should therefore be documented before using these values as range telemetry.

With a declared world-frame origin and axes, slant range can be computed as $R_s=\|\hat{\X}\|_2$. If $Y$ is the vertical axis and $X$--$Z$ define the horizontal plane, ground range is $R_g=\sqrt{X^2+Z^2}$, azimuth is $\alpha=\operatorname{atan2}(X,Z)$, and elevation is $\beta=\operatorname{atan2}(Y,R_g)$. The acceleration magnitude expressed in standard-gravity units is $n_g=\|\mathbf{a}\|_2/g_0$, where $g_0$ is standard gravity; this is a kinematic acceleration magnitude, not necessarily the specific force measured by an accelerometer. Mach number requires an environmental speed-of-sound value: $\mathrm{Ma}=\|\mathbf{V}\|_2/a_{\mathrm{sound}}$.

Temporal association must use media timestamps or an explicitly synchronized acquisition timebase. When cameras are not hardware-triggered, their offset and drift must be estimated and included in the uncertainty budget. Interpolation between frames can improve temporal sampling but cannot recover information lost to exposure blur, timing error, or an inadequate frame rate.

\subsection{Proposed Physical Validation Protocol}
The physical test-range procedure below is a proposed protocol, not a completed experiment in this manuscript:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textit{Static calibration:} survey a non-coplanar 3-D control fixture using a calibrated reference instrument; report control-point uncertainty and use separate calibration and validation points.
  \item \textit{Dynamic reference:} track a target moving on a stage or trajectory with an independently measured position-time reference. Synchronize exposure using hardware triggers where possible and record any residual time offset.
  \item \textit{Metric evaluation:} report component-wise bias, $\RMSE_{3D}=\sqrt{N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\|\hat{\X}_i-\X_{\mathrm{ref},i}\|_2^2}$, percentile errors, valid/rejected-point counts, and uncertainty intervals. Do not report only accepted points without also reporting the rejection rate.
  \item \textit{Stress testing:} vary target distance, ray angle, image-point precision, lighting, and motion speed. Preserve raw observations, camera calibration identifiers, timestamps, residuals, rejected points, and reference measurements to permit independent analysis.
\end{enumerate}
This protocol is needed to quantify physical accuracy and determine whether the configured thresholds remain appropriate over the intended measurement volume.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
The numerical design addresses two distinct failure sources. Coordinate normalization improves the scale of the calibration problem, while orthogonal factorization avoids the explicit condition-number squaring associated with normal equations. These choices improve the stability of the computations but do not resolve weak camera geometry or inaccurate observations. Ray-angle gating helps detect poorly constrained depth, and reprojection checks identify some calibration or correspondence failures; neither alone is a substitute for uncertainty analysis.

The evidence currently available has three limitations. First, cross-implementation differences measure agreement for the tested cases, not ground-truth accuracy. Second, the synthetic sensitivity values need a complete parameter record and repeated-trial statistics before they can support a general comparative claim. Third, the proposed physical validation protocol has not yet supplied measured results. Thus, this manuscript reports an algorithm and preliminary numerical evidence, rather than a completed field validation of range-tracking accuracy.

\section{Conclusion}
A two-view 3-D reconstruction pipeline has been presented using isotropic normalization for DLT calibration, compact QR--Jacobi SVD null-vector estimation, Brown--Conrady distortion inversion, and inhomogeneous Householder QR triangulation. Calibration diagnostics distinguish non-null subspace conditioning from null-space separation, while point acceptance uses spectral conditioning, ray-intersection angle, and reprojection error. Preliminary software comparisons show close agreement between two implementations, and supplied synthetic results illustrate the effect of reduced ray angle on reconstruction error. These findings support the numerical design but do not establish physical accuracy. The next step is a fully specified synthetic study and independent static and dynamic range validation with synchronized cameras and a surveyed reference.

